<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gang Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ganga Canal (Bikaner)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gang Canal is an irrigation canal in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganga-singh">Ganga Singh</a> of Bikaner and opened in 1927. It carries water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozpur">Ferozepur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> into the desert of northern Bikaner, where it turned the area around Sri Ganganagar into productive farmland. It was one of the largest irrigation works of any Indian princely state.</p></div>
